A||N||27||grinthock||Multiple Processor Discussion||||1||
Z||000000||grinthock||11-02-2001||08:24 PM||||Ok here it goes......<P><BR>The problem with multiple processors is it requires an OS that supports it.<P>NT 4 Server, 2000 Corp Pro, 2000 Adv Server, XP Corp pro, or of course Linux.<P>BUT THEN there is the software, if it's not multi threaded then 2 processors won't really help.  PLUS for REAL opimization you really need software that is BUILT for not only multiple threads but multiple processors as WELL.<P>Honestly?  Multiple processors for the average man ain't worth it... Neither is 2 GIGHERTZ!  I upgraded my celeron 400 to 1.4 GHZ, and saw little speed increase....  i am talking a noticable diff.  Boot up times are about the same, the RAM makes the diff...  <P>OK if I do a bench it shows, if I load a program it shows, do some photoshop manip, Encoding video, audio/mp3, Processes, stuff that is processing data and doesn't require a lot of hard drive accewss.  Hard drive that's what slows things, I am running Ultra 160/SCSI drives 15K RPM, to make use of the processor time.<P>Part of my 2 cents if someone else replies I'll comment.<P>------------------<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fieroworld.com" TARGET=_blank>www.fieroworld.com</A>  The place to go for Fiero INFO! <BR>Home of my 87 GT and 85 GT<BR>Justin C.<BR>Engineer, Designer and<BR>Technical Trainer for Nortel Networks Business Products.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Raydar||11-02-2001||09:29 PM||||I'm running a BP6 with dual Celery 533s and 256 MB of RAM. Had them OC'd to 600, but didn't really run that much faster. Just  warmer.<P>This was the first 'puter I ever built, and I just wanted to see if I could do it while producing the minimum amount of smoke.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Actually, my previous computer was a Pentium 133. State of the Stone Age art.<BR>I decided I wanted to burn some CDs (*not* coasters). Figured the best way to do that, and still be able to do other stuff at the same time, was to have dual processors and a bunch of RAM. This was before I found out about Plextor burn-proof technology.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><P>I run W2K Pro, but plain old NT4 Workstation supports dual processors, too. <P>Expensive overkill? Sure. <BR>But so is anything larger than a pushrod 3.4. <BR>Don't know many people that would let that stop them. <P>Next one will prolly be a VP6 with dual 1GHz PIIIs. Unless ABIT comes out with an even better board, maybe for Pentium 4s. <BR>But that's waaaay down the list of priorities.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Some mornings, it just isn't worth chewing through the restraints.</FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000002||ray b||11-02-2001||09:32 PM||||wife runs photoshop and we need a upgrade fron old k6 so thinking about a dual athlon rig will two slow cheap athlon beat one fast one for about the same $$$ ??<BR>is the scsi that much faster than UDMA HD's?<BR>does raid increase speeds or just muli disks?<P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||1
Z||000003||frontal lobe||11-02-2001||10:48 PM||||ray b, if you use 2 hard drives in raid performance mode (can't remember if it's 0 or 1), you will speed things up considerably.  It won't touch grinthocks scsi's, but definitely faster.<P>I am pretty sure one fast athlon will beat 2 slower ones in a multiprocessor setup, and even the fast athlons are pretty inexpensive.  They are a GREAT bargain.  TIME TO UPGRADE, ray b!!  Photoshop on a k6...life is too short, ray b!||||reg||1
Z||000004||grinthock||11-03-2001||01:45 AM||||Consider this, 2 600 mhz processors IS NOT as fast as 1 1.2mhz, because you can't run a process on 2 processors.<P><BR>As for SCSI, the reason it's fast is, it has it's own controller CPU on theboard, most IDE controllers usethe system CPU, bogging it down, I have UDMA/100 Raid in my other machine STILL isn't as fast as the ultra 160.  The ultra 160 was CRAZY expensive, but it's SOFAST!!!<P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Standard||11-03-2001||01:54 AM||||RAID 0 is striping, which is what gives you the speed increase.  Basic explanation.. all the even bits go to one drive, odd bits go to the other.<P>RAID 1 is mirroring.  Just like the name, the second drive is used to make a mirror image of the first, incase the first one fails.<P>Of course, you could get 4 drives and do RAID 0+1.  Speed and reliability!  There's a few other RAID modes but only SCSI RAID controllers support them, SFAIK.<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig_pic_full.jpg">          <BR><FONT COLOR="red">1988 Quad 4 Coupe 5 speed</FONT><BR><FONT SIZE="1"><FONT COLOR="white">1986 GT 5 speed, 1984 SC Auto</FONT><BR><FONT COLOR="blue">AOL: LastMinuteBastrd  AIM: SavedbyJebus</FONT></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000006||grinthock||11-03-2001||02:31 AM||||Standard -- Not True!  There are Raid 10 IDE cards out there (that's raid 1 + 0  === Striping + mirror), Raid 5 is my personal fav, cheap to do on IDE (with some hardware hackin), fast on read's where it counts.<P>Problem with raid 1 is if you lose a drive, it's ALL gone.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Formula88||11-03-2001||02:42 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by grinthock:<BR><B>Standard -- Not True!  There are Raid 10 IDE cards out there (that's raid 1 + 0  === Striping + mirror), Raid 5 is my personal fav, cheap to do on IDE (with some hardware hackin), fast on read's where it counts.<P>Problem with raid 1 is if you lose a drive, it's ALL gone.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>How do you do RAID 5 on IDE?  The only way I've seen it done is with special SCSI RAID controllers.  ||||reg||5
Z||000008||Mach10||11-03-2001||03:43 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by grinthock:<BR><B>Ok here it goes......<P><BR>The problem with multiple processors is it requires an OS that supports it.<P>NT 4 Server, 2000 Corp Pro, 2000 Adv Server, XP Corp pro, or of course Linux.<P>BUT THEN there is the software, if it's not multi threaded then 2 processors won't really help.  PLUS for REAL opimization you really need software that is BUILT for not only multiple threads but multiple processors as WELL.<P>Honestly?  Multiple processors for the average man ain't worth it... Neither is 2 GIGHERTZ!  I upgraded my celeron 400 to 1.4 GHZ, and saw little speed increase....  i am talking a noticable diff.  Boot up times are about the same, the RAM makes the diff...  <P>OK if I do a bench it shows, if I load a program it shows, do some photoshop manip, Encoding video, audio/mp3, Processes, stuff that is processing data and doesn't require a lot of hard drive accewss.  Hard drive that's what slows things, I am running Ultra 160/SCSI drives 15K RPM, to make use of the processor time.<P>Part of my 2 cents if someone else replies I'll comment.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>It's the Kernel that needs to be multi-threaded. The OS should be responsible for splitting the load over both SMPs. Admittedly, this is not as efficient as writing the program as multi-threaded for SMP use...||||reg||10
Z||000009||grinthock||11-03-2001||08:43 AM||||If the Kernel is support SMP (Symetric multi processing) then it will split up the individual processes itself to the different processors to try to speed things up.  But it couldn't really split up one process across both to acheive that speed.  it's not like SLI (Scan line interleave) on video cards.<P><BR>Sorry I alwasy get raid 0/1 swapped.  <P>Promise Technology (www.promise.com) is and always has been AMAZING when it comes to performance IDE controllers.  They have a Ultra Ata/100 raid card now that does radio 0,1,3,5 if you want!<P>VERY nice setup, I use a promise raid controller in <A HREF="http://www.fieroworld.com" TARGET=_blank>www.fieroworld.com</A>  's webside, makes the site access a tad faster, running just a plain old stripe.<P>the AMD MP platform was talking about doing SMP within the motherboard, so the system wouldn't really see 2 processors and the motherboard would split up the jobs itself, meaning running windows 98/ME would even be sped up by that, first time ANY cpu maker has suggested hardware based SMP controls.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Mach10||11-03-2001||01:28 PM||||Yes, you can't split up a single thread to two processors... But besides 'hello world' where are you going to find a single-threaded program in a 32/64bit environment?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>||||reg||12
Z||000011||beer_stud_21||11-04-2001||01:31 AM||||Not that any of you PC users care. But Mac OS X supports SMP with up to 32 processors.  Has anyone here tried the now defunct BeOS?  It had SMP support and was VERY fast even on older PC's and Macs.  I know people who are totally die hard BeOS fans.  I don't know what they are going to do now that Be got bought and killed by palm though.<P>-Marlin||||reg||1
Z||000012||p4n1c||11-04-2001||02:22 PM||||BeOS rules and you don't have to worry about programs not supporting SMP because the entire OS and programs written for it are multi-threaded.  To bad it didn't finish it's job as the ultimate media OS.<P>Programming in BeOS is a cinch.  Only downside of BeOS is lack of drivers, high end multimedia programs(like Premiere and Photoshop for multimedia(I think one company made a Premiere killer for BeOS, but they dropped it)) and the fact that they never released the new OpenGL implementation or BONE.||||reg||1
Z||000013||grinthock||11-04-2001||10:51 PM||||BeOS was a GREAT OS!  I ran it for awhile just to learn/try it out.  It's very Mac like.  SMP and graphics support were amazing.  BUT I do'nt think it supported splitting single processes across processors.<P>Nice thing about software like 3DSMax and AutoCAD is it multi threads itself and then send individual process threads to each processor, so in THAT case 2 processors = not 200% processor speed about %175 so it DOES make a big deal.||||reg||1
Z||000014||LarryB||11-05-2001||12:02 AM||||Irix scales to 128 processors, Solaris (currently) to 106 processors. Both (as well as Digital Unix/Tru64) are true SMP implementations.  Linux strictly speaking isn't quite there yet, and compared to any of these, the capabilities of the Micro$oft products is a joke.<P>BTW, multithreading does not necessarily scale particularly well.  Performance improvements are also a function of the quality of the compiler and the thread-safe libraries as well as the specific application being run.  <P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000015||artherd||11-05-2001||12:39 AM||||Hey Larry, have I found another SGI nut?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>PS: I've seen IRIX systems running a single kernel image (ie, ONE system, no 'splits') on a 2048processor Orgin2000.  Yeah, 2048porcessors.  The bandwidth of these systems scales as you add things, it's all interconnected and switched, like neurons in a brain.  There is no 'buss'.   I've also seen a smaller (256procs) Orgin2k with a bunch of GSN cards doing in excess of 1.5GBytes/second over a TCP *network* (then to a bunch of FC Ciprico RAIDs)...<BR> <A HREF="http://www.cartsys.com/hinv/colour.txt" TARGET=_blank>http://www.cartsys.com/hinv/colour.txt</A>  &lt;-my 'hottest' sgi  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>BeOS was/is truely great.  I don't belive it lets you code a single-threaded app (at the very least interface/runtimes were threaded by default)  Apple should have bought them, good god... (typing this from an OSX Powerbook)<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P><BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by LarryB:<BR><B>Irix scales to 128 processors, Solaris (currently) to 106 processors. Both (as well as Digital Unix/Tru64) are true SMP implementations.  Linux strictly speaking isn't quite there yet, and compared to any of these, the capabilities of the Micro$oft products is a joke.<P>BTW, multithreading does not necessarily scale particularly well.  Performance improvements are also a function of the quality of the compiler and the thread-safe libraries as well as the specific application being run.  <P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">  Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000016||Standard||11-05-2001||12:49 AM||||A little bit of a late reply, but...<P>I guess I was a little unclear.  I know IDE RAID supports 0, 1, and 0+1, seeing as my ABIT KT7A-RAID supports those modes.  But I didn't know if IDE supported any other modes.||||reg||1
Z||000017||grinthock||11-05-2001||07:38 AM||||The IDE standard doesn't really support RAID, it's the controller, they make RAID controllers to run any raid level on IDE.  Your board does 0 and 1, most do, RAID 5 on IDE is fairly new.<P>Best implementation of SMP I ever saw was the RenderDRIVE!  32 RISC based processors in a Linux box acting as a rendering engine off of 3DSMax.||||reg||1
Z||000018||p4n1c||11-05-2001||08:43 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by grinthock:<BR><B>BeOS was a GREAT OS!  I ran it for awhile just to learn/try it out.  It's very Mac like.  SMP and graphics support were amazing.  BUT I do'nt think it supported splitting single processes across processors.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Actually all processes are split across processors, depending on demand.  It's a true multi-threaded OS.  You can have a single process running along multiple threads.  <P>SMP was a throw in for Linux and the Micro$oft OSs, BeOS was designed around it.  Take file copying for example.  You can have multiple file copies going on without a system slow down and if you wanted to, just pause the file copy and resume it later by using the Play/Pause buttons.<P>BeOS was a great OS that never took off.  It was designed from the ground up for media operations, which is why it was designed to handle multi-processors from the start.||||reg||1
Z||000019||grinthock||11-05-2001||09:20 AM||||P4N1C - Really?  didn't realize, I wonder if they have added support in it for SIMD, MMX, 3dNOW2 and those types of accelerators, I know MMX, but my new P4 has some accelerators I wouldn't mind see'in if it helped.<P>It's sad that development and support for BeOS died.||||reg||1
Z||000020||LarryB||11-05-2001||11:58 AM||||Not so much an SGI nut, but I've supported them for 3D MCAD & solids modeling applications, web servers, and Oracle servers.  Good stable machines (except for the power supply problems with the Challenge S), stable and reasonably-laid-out OS, and stellar after-sales support. <P>It's common knowledge that the SGIs were outstanding for graphics applications, less so that they were good web servers (way better than Solaris before Sun rewrote the IP code for Solaris 2.6) and even less so as a great Oracle platform.  From a performance and stability standpoint IMO IRIX deserved better than to be considered an Oracle tier-3 platform. (BTW in other circles I'm a well-known Solaris bigot.) About the only things I could knock SGI for was the need to license products that should have been bundled with the base OS, like NFS support, and the relative lack of support for 3rd party applications outside of the graphics arena.<P>The sales rep I dealt with at SGI (his name is Brian Rull, last I heard he was still with them) was the absolute BEST sales rep for ANY product I've ever dealt with, hands down. <P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by artherd:<BR><B>Hey Larry, have I found another SGI nut?   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>PS: I've seen IRIX systems running a single kernel image (ie, ONE system, no 'splits') on a 2048processor Orgin2000.  Yeah, 2048porcessors.  The bandwidth of these systems scales as you add things, it's all interconnected and switched, like neurons in a brain.  There is no 'buss'.   I've also seen a smaller (256procs) Orgin2k with a bunch of GSN cards doing in excess of 1.5GBytes/second over a TCP *network* (then to a bunch of FC Ciprico RAIDs)...<BR> <A HREF="http://www.cartsys.com/hinv/colour.txt" TARGET=_blank>http://www.cartsys.com/hinv/colour.txt</A>  &lt;-my 'hottest' sgi   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>BeOS was/is truely great.  I don't belive it lets you code a single-threaded app (at the very least interface/runtimes were threaded by default)  Apple should have bought them, good god... (typing this from an OSX Powerbook)<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P><BR> <P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||1
Z||000021||p4n1c||11-05-2001||02:06 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by grinthock:<BR><B>P4N1C - Really?  didn't realize, I wonder if they have added support in it for SIMD, MMX, 3dNOW2 and those types of accelerators, I know MMX, but my new P4 has some accelerators I wouldn't mind see'in if it helped.<P>It's sad that development and support for BeOS died.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I doubt they have any support for anything after the P3, I know definitely MMX and maybe SIMD and 3DNow2, but nothing for the P4 or Athlon probably.  I think the last patch was release for BeOS was 5.0.3 and that was just a security update release August 4, 2000.<P>BeIA was dropped completely, noone knows what is going to happen with BeOS.  Some developers are working on cloning it into a free/open source OS.||||reg||1
Z||000022||Slammed Fiero||11-05-2001||03:17 PM||||I can't find my other thread on CPU cooling so I will post this here.<P>As some of you know I was having a noise problem with my new computers dragon orb 7500.<P>I ordered a silverado fan setup. while I was waiting for it (still am) I lined the inside of the case with dynamat.<P>It cut the noise in half! Case temp went up to 29 Deg  , CPU temp went up 3 deg on average.<P>Seems like a waste that I ordered the silverado..But I'm sure I can find a use for it. <P>JM ||||reg||1
Z||000023||grinthock||11-05-2001||06:24 PM||||HMMMM Never thought to DynaMat my case...  PRoblem is, I get noise from the fan's on the front and rear, not so much my cpu fan.||||reg||1
Z||000024||theogre||11-05-2001||08:58 PM||||If I remember right,,, unless Intel and AMD have made major changes to multiprocessing recently, 2 processors doesn't equal twice the performance. More processors yet don't help as much as people think either. <P>I'm not real sure which method AMD went with...<P>Intel, The first processor can be jammed but you're lucky when the second processor runs a half load. Processors three and four get even worse utilization. This assumes all the software supports everything. "common" software may get forced off to run on the second processor but unless optimized to multiprocessor machines it won't be pretty.<P>SCSI, unlike IDE, involves very inteligent devices on the chain. The controller and the drives all have their own processors, scratch RAM, and caches.<P>SCSI is fast because the controller can be pushing the next data block to a drive while the previous drive's local CPU is managing the actual disk write on the last block. SCSI controllers are capable of using the entire bus bandwidth even before you get into raid. Current SCSI standards allow many drives on a single cable and cable lenghts of 3-12 meters depending on the interface used.<P>IDE drives can be used to create Raid 5 arrays but there are a number of limitations on them. For one thing, All the IDE raid controllers look like octopie. drives and cables every where. This is because IDE's hardware and electrical specs aren't made for raid.<P>In addition to speed, Servers use SCSI raid for two reasons.<P>Almost all SCSI raid controllers support raid 5 with "hot spares". If an active drive quits, the raid controller spins up a parked spare and imediatly recreates the dead drive. The array slows durring this operation but does NOT go down.<P>Many also support hot swap with appropriate drive mounting hardware. (This is one of the functions of the SCSI SCA interface on drives...) Hot Swap allows you to yank a dead drive, then plug in a fresh one, which the controller will then spin up and use to recreate a failed drive.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">   <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT><p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 11-05-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000025||artherd||11-06-2001||03:06 AM||||Cool, I work on them myself (own an up and coming visual effects company) and also support them in in-flight specialised video realtime applications.<P>Yeah, they're good, fast, and *stable* as heck.<P>Take a machine designed from the ground up to just pump pixels at you at a blinding rate, pull the GFX card, maybe toss in a few more processors, and all of the sudden you have a world class server.  <P>At one point, actually for a while, SGI heald a record at Oracle for the highest trnasaction rate ever tested on any machine.  Not sure if this is still the case,  but Oracle on SGI has always been a smooth and fast cadged monster, little known  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>(PS: my webservers are lowly indigo^2s actually, and one has an uptime of over a year... they're still rockin' fast.)<P>Hrm, I know a Brian in sales (only by first name) might be the same guy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>I've played with some SUN kit too, but it just hasn't captured my imagination like SGI did.  Solaris does have user-level secuirty going for it though (I would never even think of opening shell access to an IRIX box on the internet level, there've been improvements, but the philosophy of truely public security just isn't there.)<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by LarryB:<BR><B>Not so much an SGI nut, but I've supported them for 3D MCAD & solids modeling applications, web servers, and Oracle servers.  Good stable machines (except for the power supply problems with the Challenge S), stable and reasonably-laid-out OS, and stellar after-sales support. <P>It's common knowledge that the SGIs were outstanding for graphics applications, less so that they were good web servers (way better than Solaris before Sun rewrote the IP code for Solaris 2.6) and even less so as a great Oracle platform.  From a performance and stability standpoint IMO IRIX deserved better than to be considered an Oracle tier-3 platform. (BTW in other circles I'm a well-known Solaris bigot.) About the only things I could knock SGI for was the need to license products that should have been bundled with the base OS, like NFS support, and the relative lack of support for 3rd party applications outside of the graphics arena.<P>The sales rep I dealt with at SGI (his name is Brian Rull, last I heard he was still with them) was the absolute BEST sales rep for ANY product I've ever dealt with, hands down. <P> </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000026||Flamberge||11-06-2001||04:21 AM||||Well I thought I would throw my hat in the ring. <P>I have a Dual PIII 800 system with 512 Megs RAM and RAID-ed 20 Gig HDs. The dual processor system comes in handy as I do a lot with 3d studio max, photoshop, premiere, after effects, blah blah blah. Win2000 Professional works great too (as does the dual monitor gforce.)<P>Now that I am done bragging, let me say I am in serious debt for the system I bought, though it is helping me through the rest of school.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P><P>------------------<BR>- Flamberge<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://flamberge.iwarp.com/Serra3sm.jpg"> ||||reg||12
Z||000027||LarryB||11-06-2001||08:15 AM||||With well-written drivers, a good scsi implementation can deliver on the order of 70% utilization of the bus in the real world, which is outstanding.<P>The other reason for SCSI's potential for superior performance is that their intelligence allows them to support what is known as tagged command queueing, also sometimes referred to as command tagged queueing.  If the drivers also support it (most do), tagged command queueing allows a hard drive to accept multiple commands and queue them up to be executed in order; yet because they are intelligent devices the drive electronics can reorder the queue on the fly in order to improve performance.  This potential is often wasted on single-user machines but is useful on multiuser boxes, which is why SCSI is still king for server environments.<P>SCSI also supported DMA long before it was available for IDE but on newer machines and drives, and with the newer operating systems that support DMA on IDE devices, this is no longer a significant issue.<P>Of course, the ability to hang a lot of devices of a single bus doesn't hurt either. <P>As far as scalability, it's also a function of the operating system and of the specific application as well as the hardware.  Ad an example, the old Pentium Pro processors with 256K cache scaled beautifully to 2 CPUs, did OK with 3, but pooped out by the 4th processor.  The individual processor caches were too small.  Substituting them for the redesigned processors with 1MB onboard cache addressed that issue nicely....for those applications that could take advantage of it.<P>I do know the scalability of Solaris through 64 processors is claimed to be &gt; 80%.  Earlier versions claimed scalability through 8 CPUs of ~93%.  Again, this would be application-specific. <P><BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by theogre:<BR><B>If I remember right,,, unless Intel and AMD have made major changes to multiprocessing recently, 2 processors doesn't equal twice the performance. More processors yet don't help as much as people think either. <P>I'm not real sure which method AMD went with...<P>Intel, The first processor can be jammed but you're lucky when the second processor runs a half load. Processors three and four get even worse utilization. This assumes all the software supports everything. "common" software may get forced off to run on the second processor but unless optimized to multiprocessor machines it won't be pretty.<P>SCSI, unlike IDE, involves very inteligent devices on the chain. The controller and the drives all have their own processors, scratch RAM, and caches.<P>SCSI is fast because the controller can be pushing the next data block to a drive while the previous drive's local CPU is managing the actual disk write on the last block. SCSI controllers are capable of using the entire bus bandwidth even before you get into raid. Current SCSI standards allow many drives on a single cable and cable lenghts of 3-12 meters depending on the interface used.<P>IDE drives can be used to create Raid 5 arrays but there are a number of limitations on them. For one thing, All the IDE raid controllers look like octopie. drives and cables every where. This is because IDE's hardware and electrical specs aren't made for raid.<P>In addition to speed, Servers use SCSI raid for two reasons.<P>Almost all SCSI raid controllers support raid 5 with "hot spares". If an active drive quits, the raid controller spins up a parked spare and imediatly recreates the dead drive. The array slows durring this operation but does NOT go down.<P>Many also support hot swap with appropriate drive mounting hardware. (This is one of the functions of the SCSI SCA interface on drives...) Hot Swap allows you to yank a dead drive, then plug in a fresh one, which the controller will then spin up and use to recreate a failed drive.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||1
